+NOTES = """
+Paper basis: Section II.A defines an n-variable Boolean function on F_2^n, its
+truth-table ordering, Walsh transform (Eq. 3), nonlinearity (Eq. 4), and bentness.
+The same section explicitly separates deterministic algebraic constructions from
+random and heuristic search.  Section II.B writes Walsh evaluation as a Hadamard
+matrix multiplication (Eq. 5) and states that bentness is exactly flat absolute
+Walsh spectrum.  Those are the native objects used here.
+
+Step-0 choice: asking merely for any bent function was rejected.  A direct
+Maiorana--McFarland formula would solve that request, and the paper's own n=4
+experiment reports 95.86 percent bent outputs at its best coupling; Section VI
+then improves near-optima by greedy hill climbing.  Such a family could not make
+an honest Track-A claim.  This module instead declares Track B and asks for the
+compact parameters of a promised function from its complete exact spectrum.
+
+Certificate production: sample the permutation and offset first.  For
+f(x,y)=x dot pi(y) XOR g(y), summing over x proves directly that
+W_f(a,b)=2^m*(-1)^(g(y)+b dot y), where y=pi^{-1}(a).  make_instance uses this
+identity and never solves the emitted instance.
+
+What makes it easy: a fast Walsh--Hadamard transform is self-inverse up to the
+factor 2^n, so it reconstructs the truth table in O(n*2^n); m+1 probes per y then
+extract pi and g.  The family is hard only in the declared no-tool-compression
+sense.  The compact route notices that every normalized row is a signed Walsh
+character and reads y from the base and unit-coordinate signs.
+
+Attack controls: permutations are uniform subject only to being non-affine for
+m>=3, so identity, row-order greedy, and an affine extrapolation from basis
+points fail.  Random restart samples the exact stated certificate language.
+Plants and random candidates use the same permutation/offset marginals except
+for the disclosed non-affine conditioning; no magnitude or position outlier is
+inserted.  The successful inverse-FWHT algorithm is reported separately as the
+Track-B reference algorithm, never disguised as a failed attack.
+""".strip()
